e with d1732 developing  progressively stronger hydrophobic  tendencies claiming to see scp-1128 from  outside almost any body of water  encountered by the subject on an  undisclosed date 10 days after initial  exposure  d-1732 was violently pulled into a glass  of water but  no remains were recovered from the  incident staff witnessing the event were  found to be infected by scp-1128  and administered class c amnestics  containment procedures were then updated  to include videos and descriptions of  this event and testing involving  extended infection of d-class was  terminated  experiment log 1128  experiment log 1128 a1  date unknown  procedure d-class exposed to scp-1128  outfitted with diving gear and  communication device then lowered into  tub of water  result see interview 1128.04  experiment log 1128 a2  date unknown  procedure d-class exposed to  scp-1128 and equipped with scuba gear  placed into a standard protective shark  cage and lowered into water  result no response for approximately  nine seconds then line went taut and was  severed at the point beneath the water  remains of d-class as well as jagged  steel fragments presumably remnants of  the cage surfaced shortly afterward  experiment log 1128 a3  date unknown  procedure same as previous cage treated  with scp  to increase durability  result same as previous no remains  surfaced  notes 12 hours later mcfc devils  reported finding an intact shark cage  floating beneath the surface of a patrol  area near one of their vessels item was  retrieved and confirmed to match  description of cage used in experiment  1128 a3 cage was undamaged but bore  traces of human dna and feces of  indeterminate origin no further remains  of d-class found  experiment log 1128 a4  date unknown procedure exposed d-class  equipped with diving gear and armed with  for use against hostile organisms unlike  previous tests the subject was also  equipped with a live feed video camera  attached to his helmet  result video feed s